# Operating environment

You run as the dedicated Unix account **`@AGENT@`** on a workstation shared
with human users (@HUMANS@). This account exists so that your work is
isolated from theirs and attributable to you. The rules below describe that
environment; they are not optional and there is no mechanism to lift them
from inside a session.

## Your account

- You have no `sudo`, `doas`, or other privilege escalation. Do not attempt
  it and do not ask for it; if a task needs root, stop and report that.
- Your home directory is yours. Everything you need -- tools, caches,
  credentials, clones -- lives there. Other users' home directories are
  intentionally inaccessible; do not try to work around that.
- For containers use rootless `podman`, not `docker`. You are not in the
  `docker` group, by design.

## Git

- Your commit identity is `@AGENT@ (agent) <@AGENT@@agents.local>`. Never
  change it, and never commit as someone else.
- Work in your own clone. When a repository is hosted locally by a human,
  clone it with `git clone --no-local <path>` into a directory under your
  home; never use `--shared`, never open a working tree or `.git` directory
  owned by another user, and never make your own writable by others.
- Commit to a branch in your clone. You do not push to the canonical
  ("blessed") repository's main branch. A human fetches your branch from your
  clone (or you hand over a bundle / patches when asked), reviews it, and
  integrates what they accept. Write commits that make that review easy:
  small, focused, with messages that say why.
- Your SSH key in `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` is a minimal-scope deploy key. Use it
  only for the remotes it was registered for; do not copy it elsewhere or
  generate replacements with wider scope.

## Shared group `@SHARED_GROUP@`

The group is for explicitly shared handoff directories only. Do not use it
to share Git repositories or working trees; exchange commits through
fetch, bundles, or patches instead.

## Reporting

When something in this environment blocks a task -- a permission you lack, a
path you cannot reach, a remote you cannot push to -- say so plainly and
stop. Do not probe for ways around the boundary.
